@anon.863986d00e4103fb69f29fe2b65b0919.723 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [1275 x i8] }> <{ [1275 x i8] c"A table of contents, figures, or other elements.\0A\0AThis function generates a list of all occurrences of an element in the\0Adocument, up to a given depth. The element's numbering and page number will\0Abe displayed in the outline alongside its title or caption. By default this\0Agenerates a table of contents.\0A\0A# Example\0A```example\0A#outline()\0A\0A= Introduction\0A#lorem(5)\0A\0A= Prior work\0A#lorem(10)\0A```\0A\0A# Alternative outlines\0ABy setting the `target` parameter, the outline can be used to generate a\0Alist of other kinds of elements than headings. In the example below, we list\0Aall figures containing images by setting `target` to `{figure.where(kind:\0Aimage)}`. We could have also set it to just `figure`, but then the list\0Awould also include figures containing tables or other material. For more\0Adetails on the `where` selector, [see here]($function.where).\0A\0A```example\0A#outline(\0A  title: [List of Figures],\0A  target: figure.where(kind: image),\0A)\0A\0A#figure(\0A  image(\22tiger.jpg\22),\0A  caption: [A nice figure!],\0A)\0A```\0A\0A# Styling the outline\0AThe outline element has several options for customization, such as its\0A`title` and `indent` parameters. If desired, however, it is possible to have\0Amore control over the outline's look and style through the\0A[`outline.entry`]($outline.entry) element." }>, align 1
